The pread and pwrite functions are POSIX system calls that allow reading from and writing to a file at a specific offset without modifying the file's current offset. They are used in multithreaded environments to perform I/O operations safely, as multiple threads share the same file descriptors, helping prevent race conditions and ensuring that each operation is atomic relative to the file position.


**Syntax:**
ssize_t pread(int fd, void *buf, size_t count, off_t offset); ssize_t pwrite(int fd, const void *buf, size_t count, off_t offset);

Template specialization allows programmers to define custom implementations of class or function templates for specific types or conditions. This feature is used to optimize or modify behavior for particular data types, enabling more efficient or tailored code while maintaining generic programming capabilities. It is a core aspect of C++ template metaprogramming, facilitating compile-time customization and type-specific optimizations.


