The Rule of Five dictates that when you define any of the following special member functions for a class, you should explicitly define all five to manage resource allocation and deallocation effectively, preventing resource leaks and undefined behavior.  These functions are crucial for ensuring correct object lifecycle management, especially when dealing with dynamically allocated resources.  Failing to properly define these can lead to double-deletion, memory leaks, and subtle bugs that are difficult to track down.  The five members are: destructor (~), copy constructor (copy constructor), copy assignment operator (=), move constructor, and move assignment operator.  The Rule of Zero (defaulting to compiler-generated versions) and Rule of One (defining a custom destructor and letting the compiler handle the rest) are also valuable considerations but often less applicable in complex scenarios.


**Syntax:**
Example for a class managing a dynamically allocated array:

```cpp
class MyData {
private:
  int *data;
  size_t size;
public:
  MyData(size_t s) : size(s), data(new int[s]){}
  ~MyData() { delete[] data; }
  MyData(const MyData& other) : size(other.size), data(new int[other.size]) {
    std::copy(other.data, other.data + other.size, data);
  }
  MyData& operator=(const MyData& other) {
    if (this != &other) {
      delete[] data;
      size = other.size;
      data = new int[other.size];
      std::copy(other.data, other.data + other.size, data);
    }
    return *this;
  }
  //Move constructor and move assignment operator would go here for efficiency
  // ... other methods ...
};
```
**Practical Usage Notes:**  Prioritize using smart pointers (e.g., `std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) to automatically manage dynamically allocated memory whenever possible.  This significantly reduces the chance of memory leaks and simplifies the code.  Explicitly defining the five members is often necessary only when dealing with raw pointers and custom resource management.

