The C++ Standard Template Library (STL) provides a rich set of containers, such as `vector`, `list`, `deque`, `set`, `map`, etc.  Choosing the right container significantly impacts performance and code readability. Understanding their characteristics and usage scenarios is crucial for writing efficient and maintainable C++ code.  A common pitfall is using the wrong container for a given task, leading to performance bottlenecks or overly complex code. For example, using `vector` for frequent insertions/deletions in the middle is inefficient compared to `list` or `deque`. Using `std::map` for fast lookups by key is more efficient than searching through a `vector`.


