C++ supports both compile-time and runtime polymorphism.  Compile-time polymorphism, achieved through function overloading and templates, resolves function calls at compile time.  Runtime polymorphism, achieved through virtual functions and inheritance, resolves function calls at runtime based on the object's dynamic type. Understanding the difference is crucial for writing efficient and correct code.  Choosing the wrong type of polymorphism can lead to unexpected behavior or performance issues.


**Syntax:**
Compile-time polymorphism:

```c++
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
```

Runtime polymorphism (using virtual functions):

```c++
class Base {
public:
  virtual double net_price() { return 10.0; }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
  double net_price() override { return 12.0; }
};
```


**Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
class Shape {
public:
    virtual double area() { return 0.0; }
};
class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}
    double area() override { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }
private:
    double radius;
};
class Square : public Shape {
public:
    Square(double s) : side(s) {}
    double area() override { return side * side; }
private:
    double side;
};
int main() {
    std::vector<Shape*> shapes;
shapes.push_back(new Circle(5));
shapes.push_back(new Square(4));
    double totalArea = 0.0;
    for (Shape* shape : shapes) {
        totalArea += shape->area(); // Runtime polymorphism in action
    }
    std::cout << "Total area: " << totalArea << std::endl;
    //Remember to delete dynamically allocated objects to prevent memory leaks!
    for (Shape* shape : shapes) {
        delete shape;
    }
    return 0;
}

```

