Practical usage notes: Use 'throw' to raise exceptions, prefer standard exceptions like std::runtime_error, and always include necessary headers like <exception>. In real-world scenarios, nest try-catch blocks for modular error handling and combine with smart pointers to ensure resource safety.

Pointers are variables that store memory addresses.  In C, they are fundamental for manipulating data efficiently, particularly when working with arrays and dynamically allocated memory. Arrays are contiguous blocks of memory holding elements of the same data type. Pointers and arrays are closely related in C because an array name decays into a pointer to its first element in most contexts. This allows for flexible and efficient data access and manipulation.


**Syntax:**
Datatype *pointer_name; 

/* Array declaration */
